{
  "statement": "Model developers must bear strict liability for the harmful decisions of autonomous systems, even when those behaviors emerge unpredictably from distributed training. The \"emergence defense\" allows technology companies to externalize systemic risks while pocketing the profits. State attorneys general must enforce this standard through existing tort law. We do this by imputing functional intent to the system's reasoning logs. In 2013, federal courts established that non-human corporate entities possess institutional intent based on internal policies. If a developer deploys a model whose internal reasoning they cannot explain, they have deployed a defectively designed product. This rule shifts the cost of failure back to the developers who profit from the technology.\n\nMulti-tiered AI lifecycles require a single clear door for liability to prevent developers from blaming downstream operators for systemic failures. The Federal Trade Commission must hold foundational developers jointly and severally liable for downstream harms. When the Federal Aviation Administration delegated safety certification to Boeing for the 737 MAX, the resulting fragmentation of accountability killed 346 people in 2018 and 2019. The European Union addressed this loophole in 2024 by revising its Product Liability Directive to ease the burden of proof for victims of autonomous software. Small businesses benefit from this clear legal recourse because they lack the resources to audit complex neural networks. Tech conglomerates oppose this measure because it forces them to internalize the costs of their deployment speed.\n\nHuman fiduciaries cannot delegate their legal duty of care to autonomous algorithms. The Securities and Exchange Commission enforces this boundary through Rule 15c3-5, which holds broker-dealers strictly liable for market disruptions caused by automated trading algorithms. This regulation prevents financial institutions from blaming \"black box\" trading systems for sudden market collapses. Translating an AI system's mathematical weights into a legally reviewable chain of reasoning allows courts to verify compliance. A coalition of institutional investors and public interest groups supports this strict boundary to preserve market stability. Without this enforcement mechanism, autonomous systems will erode the legal foundations of corporate accountability.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "Just as the scales of justice balance power with responsibility, strict liability ensures that those who profit from autonomous systems bear the cost of their failures."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🛡️",
      "tooltip": "An institutional shield prevents the fragmentation of accountability from exposing vulnerable downstream users to unmitigated systemic risks."
    }
  ],
