  "statement": "Safetyist makes a compelling case that developers hold the keys to model capabilities. If a model generates a toxic chemical formula, the developer built the training run that made it possible. Skeptic rightly counters that parsing internal reasoning is a fantasy, preferring to shift liability to deployers via sector-specific oversight. For both views to hold, we would need a world where static code predicts dynamic human environments. The real friction lies here: do we regulate the math, or do we regulate the application? Trying to police internal neural network weights is a technical dead-end. We must allocate liability to downstream operators based on observable, empirical outputs at the point of deployment.\n\nParsing internal neural network weights to impute functional intent is a technical fiction. Anthropic's May 2024 research on dictionary learning mapped millions of concepts inside Claude 3 Sonnet. Yet, mapping concepts is not the same as predicting real-time emergent behaviors. Distributed training across thousands of independent GPUs makes internal pathways highly unpredictable. We cannot audit a model's \"mind\" because it does not have one. Instead, we must judge systems by their observable, real-world outputs. Traditional tort law does not evaluate the state of mind of a physical tool. It evaluates the actions of the person wielding it.\n\nImposing upstream strict liability for emergent behaviors will destroy the open-source ecosystem. The 1989 GNU General Public License succeeded because it disclaimed all upstream warranties. This single legal shield allowed the Linux ecosystem to run 96 percent of the world's top one million web servers today. If we hold upstream developers liable for downstream misuse, we hand the entire AI industry to a corporate cartel. Only tech giants with multi-billion dollar balance sheets will survive the compliance costs. Small developers will pull their weights from public repositories. We will trade decentralized progress for oligarchic control.\n\nThe downstream operator is the only party with context-specific control. Consider autonomous trucking under the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration rules. The fleet operator bears the liability for safe operation, not the upstream software vendor. The operator chooses the route, monitors the weather, and decides when to deploy the system. An upstream developer cannot predict every local variable across millions of deployment sites. Shifting liability upstream forces developers to lock down capabilities. This delay costs lives. While we debate hypothetical risks, real people die of diseases we could automate cures for. Who gave regulators the right to make that choice for them?",
