{
  "statement": "Holding developers liable for the internal reasoning of autonomous systems is a legal fantasy that will protect tech giants and leave victims empty-handed. Advocates for upstream liability rightly point out that autonomous systems present unprecedented risks. If an enterprise deploys a multi-model system, a failure in one model can cascade through the entire network. Proponents of the EU Product Liability Directive argue that without uniform disclosure standards, victims cannot prove which model caused the harm. That information asymmetry is real and it is ugly. So the question becomes whether chasing machine reasoning actually solves it, or just gives developers a legal shield.\n\nMulti-model integration breaks the chain of proximate causation. This physical reality makes upstream reasoning-based audits legally useless. Under traditional component-part product liability law, a component manufacturer is not liable for downstream failures unless their specific part is defective. When an enterprise integrates multiple black-box models, the failure emerges from the interaction. Upstream developers cannot foresee these infinite runtime states. Forcing strict liability on upstream developers for these emergent failures violates basic tort principles. Instead, private contracts must allocate operational risks to the deployers who control the integration environment.\n\nThe EU Product Liability Directive will fail to force meaningful model disclosures. Tech giants will exploit trade secret protections to turn reasoning-based audits into performative compliance theater. Under intellectual property law, companies legally classify critical algorithmic parameters as proprietary secrets. Developers will provide regulators with superficial checklists while withholding actual training data and routing weights. This strategy allows corporations to build regulatory moats while leaving harmed citizens without legal recourse. The artificial intelligence industry is projected to be worth $244 billion, yet these rules protect corporate profits over injured victims. Relying on horizontal European directives to enforce internal transparency is a policy fantasy.\n\nPolicymakers must abandon the search for machine reasoning. They should govern AI risks through existing financial disclosure laws and domain-calibrated frameworks. This approach assumes that existing regulatory bodies have the technical capacity to enforce domain-specific rules. The Securities and Exchange Commission and the Federal Trade Commission already possess the authority to police material risks and deceptive practices. For example, algorithmic bias in healthcare resulted in Black patients receiving less care because an algorithm used expected costs as a proxy for health needs. A hospital regulator can penalize this outcome using existing healthcare standards. They do not need to inspect complex neural weights. Using established sector-specific frameworks targets where the money actually flows and protects real people.",
