 will bypass trade secret shields by mandating secure, clean-room access to model weights and training datasets. Public safety must override commercial secrecy when systemic risks threaten physical or financial infrastructure. The federal government has long maintained secure repositories for highly sensitive proprietary data. Since the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, the SEC and FINRA have audited proprietary algorithmic trading systems without leaking intellectual property. The FTC will require developers of frontier models to deposit model weights in federally secured, air-gapped clean rooms. Independent, cleared auditors will run diagnostic tools on these weights to verify safety. This mechanism protects corporate intellectual property while ensuring public safety. If a model's weights remain uninterpretable, regulators cannot verify its safety. This technical opacity means a malicious actor can exploit hidden vulnerabilities to disable critical infrastructure. This clean-room framework protects the public. Frontier AI labs bear the administrative cost of compliance.",
