attempts.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "Courts must reject process-based liability frameworks for emergent AI systems, allocating liability instead based on observable outcomes at deployment through contract-driven indemnification and corporate disclosure. Safetyists argue that clean-room access to model weights lets regulators run dynamic simulations to catch catastrophic failures before deployment. Yet, predicting emergent behavior from static weights is mathematically impossible. It is like inspecting a grain of sand to predict a landslide. This technocratic audit regime merely creates a regulatory moat for tech giants. In 2025, the U.S. Senate voted 99-1 to strip state-level AI moratoria, showing that centralized federal mandates lack local political feasibility.\n\nAccelerationists argue that a $5 million capitalization floor paired with market-priced liability insurance will naturally squeeze out reckless, under-capitalized actors. While a $5 million floor stops assetless shell companies from externalizing risk, pure market insurance is a fantasy. Insurers cannot price risks they cannot measure. Without standardized disclosures, underwriters will either underprice systemic risks—leading to insolvency—or overprice them to protect incumbents. Powerful tech monopolies will use their cash reserves to self-insure, evading accountability while smaller competitors collapse under premium costs.\n\nWe must shift compliance liability from developers to deployers using private bilateral contracts. Private contracts allow businesses to negotiate customized indemnification clauses based on specific, real-world deployment contexts. To ground these contracts, we must rely on existing securities law to mandate standardized corporate disclosures regarding AI system performance. This balances public interest with trade secrets without requiring unworkable clean-room weight audits. This framework protects the public by ensuring clear, legally enforceable lines of accountability when systems fail.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "Bilateral contracts and existing securities laws provide a balanced scale for liability, whereas unworkable technical audits tip the scales toward corporate evasion."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🛡️",
      "tooltip": "A capitalization floor acts as a shield against shell companies, but it must be paired with real-world contract enforcement rather than speculative insurance markets."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "Courts must reject process-based liability frameworks for emergent AI systems, allocating liability instead based on observable outcomes at deployment through contract-driven indemnification and corporate disclosure.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "While a $5 million floor stops assetless shell companies from externalizing risk, pure market insurance is a fantasy.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "We must shift compliance liability from developers to deployers using private bilateral contracts.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "To ground these contracts, we must rely on existing securities law to mandate standardized corporate disclosures regarding AI system performance.",
      "targets": []
    }
  ],
  "position_update": "The position has evolved from a defensive critique of centralized safety audits to an active advocacy for a hybrid, contract-driven, and securities-backed liability framework. By integrating capitalization floors with private bilateral contracts and existing corporate disclosure laws, this approach rejects both unworkable technocratic audits and unregulated market insurance."
}

